.TH SYS_BRK 2
.SH NAME
sys_brk \- grow or shrink the heap (SYS_BRK)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long sys_brk(uintptr_t end);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sys_brk
moves the end of the calling process's heap, which starts just after its
program, to
.IR end :
pages are added (zeroed) or given back. 0 only asks where the end is.
.PP
The heap may grow up to 64 GiB above its start (a language model's
weights and cache take a few GiB); the real limits are the process's
memory quota
.RB ( SPAWN_QUOTA ,
.BR sys_spawn (2))
and free memory. The pages are given at once, not on first touch: memory
given on first touch is
.BR sys_mmap (2).
.B malloc
uses this call.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The new end (or the current one, for 0), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOMEM
.I end
is below the heap's start or more than 64 GiB above it, or the quota or
the memory is exhausted (the heap is left as it was).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_mmap (2),
.BR sys_spawn (2),
.BR sys_info (2)
